Deep brain stimulation (DBS) devices are implantable medical systems that deliver controlled electrical impulses to specific areas of the brain. They are primarily used to manage movement disorders such as Parkinson's disease, essential tremor, and dystonia. Research into additional applications, including obsessive-compulsive disorder and epilepsy, is also broadening interest in this technology.

Technological improvements in rechargeable pulse generators, directional electrode leads, and MRI-conditional systems are helping improve treatment flexibility and device functionality. North America remains a leading regional market, supported by established healthcare infrastructure and access to advanced neurological care, while Asia-Pacific presents significant growth opportunities as healthcare investment and awareness of DBS therapy increase. <Cite refs={["turn821463search0"]}/>

